== A Wife’s Not Going Against Her Husband’s Will (II)

=== §. 33. #emph[Of the subjection of wives in distributing goods to charitable uses.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 54.]

Some who grant that a wife is so subject to her husband in civil matters that she must not dispose of any part of his goods at her pleasure for any civil use deny that this subjection extends to giving alms and other such charitable uses.

Before I come to settle this question, let it be remembered that it was granted before that #footnote[§. 22.] ordinary duty must give way to extraordinary need, so that relief in present necessity is not in dispute. Let it also be remembered that #footnote[§. 21.] a wife may have goods of her own; indeed, #footnote[Treatise 4, §. 54.] it will be shown that a husband ought, according to his ability, to commit something to her discretion and disposal. Out of these and such goods she is as much bound as her husband to spend something on charitable uses, and (as God gives occasion) to reach out her hand to the poor and needy.#footnote[Prov 31:20.]

Further, let this be laid down first: if a wife is forbidden or restrained by her husband, she ought to use all the good means she can, by herself and her family, to move her husband to grant her some liberty, so that she may have some opportunity to prove her merciful and charitable disposition. If she cannot prevail in this, then she ought to make known to her husband the cases of such persons as she thinks fit to be relieved, and use every argument she can to persuade him to give them some relief.

But suppose a wise, religious, merciful wife is married #footnote[The state of the question.] to a covetous worldling who, though he has sense and understanding enough to manage civil affairs and to provide for the outward, temporal condition of the house, yet has no heart to relieve the poor, and is not only unwilling to do good in that way himself, but will not allow his wife to do it. May a wife then secretly take some of the goods he has reserved for his own disposal and, absolutely without any kind of consent, distribute them to charitable uses? Or, though he expressly forbids her, may she dispose of them directly against his consent?

With reverent respect to better judgments, I think she must #footnote[#emph[Non excusabit bona intentio vxoris, etiamsi ea acciperet ad eleemosynam faciendam. Greg. Sayr. clav. reg. lib.] 9. #emph[cap.] 16. \[The wife’s good intention will not excuse her, even if she took them to give alms.\]] not (except as excepted before). For since it was proved before, in general, that she had no such liberty in disposing of goods, I cannot see how this particular end of giving alms can dispense with her general subjection “in everything,” unless there were some particular warrant for it in God’s word.

=== §. 34. #emph[Of general exhortations to works of mercy. How far they bind wives.]

#emph[Objection.] The many general exhortations to works of mercy, which are directed to all generally, without limitation to any particular persons, give wives sufficient warrant. Such as these: “Give alms,”#footnote[Luke 11:41.] “Let us do good,”#footnote[Gal 6:10.] “Do not neglect … to share with others,” etc.#footnote[Heb 13:16.]

#emph[Answer.] All these are strong motives to provoke wives to be merciful and charitable in such things as they can give by any means with their husbands’ consent, or in such things as their husbands have given them. Indeed, they are also strong motives to provoke husbands to allow them liberty to give alms. But in the case put forward they give wives no liberty. For it is a settled case, laid down by Christ himself, that works of charity must be done, and alms must be given, out of such things #footnote[Luke 11:41 τὰ ἐνόντα.] #emph[as we have,] or which are in our power to give.#footnote[#emph[Date quae habetis eleemosynam Tertul. contr. Marcion. l.] 4. 2 Cor 8:12.] Now if the husband will not give her that power, she has no power to give, and so she is excused. In this case her true will, and her faithful and earnest desire, will be accepted for the deed, according to what the apostle says: “If the willing mind is there, the gift is acceptable according to what one has, not according to what he does not have.” Many cases may be given in which inferiors are restrained from works of mercy. Suppose, for example, that a son or servant wants to visit someone sick or in prison, but his parent or master (though leave is asked) will not allow him, but charges him not to stir out of doors, or to go with him another way. Shall this son or servant do that work of mercy in spite of that charge?

=== §. 35. #emph[Of obedience to a husband in such things as he sinfully forbids.]

#emph[Objection.] 2. This restraint is not #emph[in the Lord,] but rather against him and his word; therefore a wife is not bound by it.

#emph[Answer.] Though the husband sins in restraining his wife, yet she may obey in that restraint, and that #emph[in the Lord,] because the Lord, who has commanded her to be subject “in everything,” has nowhere given her warrant not to be subject in this particular. It is expressly said in the law concerning a wife’s vows that if her husband “nullifies them after he hears of them, then he will bear her iniquity.” #footnote[Num 30:16.] Did he not then sin in restraining her, and was she not guiltless, though she yielded to his restraint? The condition between husbands and wives in this case is not unlike the case between other superiors in authority and their inferiors in subjection. Now other inferiors may lawfully abstain from such things as their governors sinfully charge them to abstain from. For suppose a son, grown to be a man, lives in his father’s house at his father’s expense and has no set portion of his own, and his father will not give him leave to spend anything on charitable uses: is he now bound to give alms? Will the curse fall on him if he does not give? I acknowledge indeed that it is a cross, both to such a son and also to a wife, to be so restrained; but not a curse or a sin. The sin and the curse lie on the heads of those who restrain them by virtue of their authority, in which they abuse their authority, as other governors may do and often do; and yet by this neither is liberty granted to subjects, nor authority taken from governors. In this comparison between a son and a wife I do not wish to be misunderstood. For I do not bring it to make the condition of a wife and of a son all one, but to show that those general precepts of giving alms may have their exceptions, as those who handle that point in particular give other examples. There must therefore be some further ground than the general command of almsgiving to prove the liberty of wives just named.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of Zipporah’s case in circumcising her son.]

#emph[Objection.] 3. A wife was made to be a help to her husband. Therefore, in the things in which he fails, she must make up for him, as Zipporah did, who performed a duty that belonged to her #footnote[Exod 4:25.] husband, and not to her.

#emph[Answer.] She may be a help in many other things, though this is out of her power; indeed, in this too she may be a great help by counsel, persuasion and other such means. The case of Zipporah was extraordinary, and one of urgent, present necessity, even to save her husband’s life. Besides, Moses was unable to do it himself, but was willing that she should do it. Now what is this to ordinary cases, and to such things as husbands are well able to do themselves, but are altogether unwilling that their wives should do? #footnote[#emph[Factum Zephorae singulare est, & extraordinarium, neque constat illud simpliciter à Deo approbatum fuisse. Simler. in Exod.] 4. \[The act of Zipporah was singular and extraordinary, and it is not established that it was approved by God without qualification.\] Luke 8:3.]

But what if Zipporah’s example in this is not warrantable? For it does not appear that it was wholly approved by God. God often removes temporary judgments for the sake of the very work that is done, though it is done sinfully in its manner. Take as an instance the repentance of Ahab, 1 Kgs 21:29.

=== §. 37. #emph[Of the wife of] Chuza’s #emph[case in ministering to Christ.]

4\. #emph[Objection.] Joanna, the wife of Chuza, Herod’s household manager, ministered to Christ out of her means without her husband’s consent.

#emph[Answer.] If this could be proved, it would be to some purpose. But this clause, #emph[without her husband’s consent] (in which the main point of the question lies), is not in the text, nor can it be gathered from it with any good probability. All the show of probability that can be given for it is that Joanna is said there to be “the wife of Herod’s household manager Chuza.” But to show that that amounts to nothing, let it be noted:

1\. That phrase does not imply that Chuza was then living. It is said that “David was the father of Solomon by Uriah’s wife,”#footnote[Matt 1:6 ἐκ τῆς τοῦ Οὐρίου.] but Uriah was not then living. Therefore the translators, for clarity’s sake, insert these words #emph[(her that had been)] the wife of Uriah. So likewise Onan is commanded to sleep with his #footnote[Gen 38:8.] “brother’s wife,” yet this does not imply that his brother was then living.

#emph[Objection.] Why should mention be made of Chuza, Herod’s household manager, if he was not then living?

#emph[Answer.] To show that Joanna was a woman of high position, so that this fruit of her faith in following Christ was all the more commended. So, in another kind, Matthew is given the title #footnote[Matt 10:3 ὁ τελώνης.] “the tax collector” after he had wholly left that office, the more to commend his faith.

I do not here directly affirm that Chuza was then dead; but for anything this phrase implies, he might have been dead.

2\. Some gather that this household manager was the royal official whose #footnote[#emph[Chemnit. Harm. Evang. cap.] 33. John 4:53.] son Christ healed, and who then believed, “he and all his household.” If he was, then there can be no doubt that his wife followed Christ with his goodwill and consent.

3\. Chuza, being Herod’s household manager, and so a man of high position and public employment, might, if he was then living, have deputed the managing of all affairs at home to his wife, as the #footnote[Prov 31:11.] husband of the good wife commended by Solomon did; and so she might have had at least a general consent.

I do not determine with certainty that any of these is expressly so; I only note them as probabilities, yet such as sufficiently overthrow the supposed liberty of a wife to give alms without any consent of her husband. For of all the probabilities, this one seems the most improbable. It can never enter my heart to imagine that Christ would give his enemies such an occasion of slander as to say that he carried other men’s wives about with him, without or against their husbands’ consent, and allowed them to spend their husbands’ goods on him. I would much rather think either that the women who followed him had no husbands living, or, if they had, that they did what they did with their husbands’ consent.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of the restraint of wives about allowance for themselves or families without their husbands’ consent.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 18. 52.]

What has been said so far concerning a wife’s subjection in disposing of goods may also be applied to other things concerning herself, her children, servants, etc. Of this I will give some examples.

A wife has no power to appoint what she pleases by herself, without or against her husband’s consent, either for her own allowance or for her family; she must rather rest satisfied with what he appoints. For he, being the head, must have the overruling say in it. Besides, he knows better what can be afforded.

#emph[Question.] What if a husband makes himself poorer than he is, and the allowance he appoints is lower than his means, and unbecoming his position and standing?

#emph[Answer.] She ought, if she possibly can, by her own earnest persuasion or any other fair means, to move him to what tends to his honor and reputation. But if she can in no way prevail, her subjection requires contentment and patience.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of a wife’s subjection to her husband about children.]#footnote[Treatise 4, §. 18.]

A wife must not, absolutely without, or directly against, her husband’s consent, order and arrange matters for the children in giving them names, clothing their bodies, appointing their callings, their places of upbringing, their marriages or their portions.

1\. As for giving names to children: besides that throughout #footnote[1\. Names.] Scripture this is for the most part laid on the husband, as on Abraham,#footnote[Gen 17:19.] on Zechariah#footnote[Luke 1:13.] and on others, and that husbands have ordinarily done so accordingly, as Adam,#footnote[Gen 5:3.] Lamech,#footnote[Gen 5:29.] Abraham#footnote[Gen 21:3.] and others, it is to be noted that when there was a difference between the man and his wife in naming a child, he giving one name and she another, the name he gave stood. Though Rachel#footnote[Gen 35:18.] named her youngest son Ben-oni, yet Benjamin (the name Jacob gave) was the child’s name. So also when Elizabeth#footnote[Luke 1:62.] told her relatives that her child’s name must be John, they would not rest in it until Zechariah had confirmed that name. Indeed, though Joseph was only the supposed father of Jesus, yet because he was the husband of Mary, the mother of Jesus, #footnote[Matt 1:21.] he was given this honor, to give the name to her child.

Where Scripture sometimes says that the mothers named their children, as Leah,#footnote[Gen 29:32.] Rachel#footnote[Gen 30:24.] and others, it is to be supposed, on the ground just named, that they had their husbands’ consent.

2\. As for appointing place and marriage, it is noted that Rebekah#footnote[Gen 27:43, #emph[etc.]] #footnote[2\. Place and marriage.] asked her husband’s consent. Though she told her son Jacob that he should go to his uncle Laban in Haran, to be kept safe there from the fury of Esau, yet she would not send him until Isaac#footnote[Gen 28:1, 2.] had given his consent to his staying there and to his taking a wife from there.

3\. As for appointing to a calling, it is noted of Hannah#footnote[1 Sam 1:11.] that, #footnote[3\. Calling.] though before her child was born she had dedicated him to the Lord by a solemn vow, yet when the child was born #footnote[#emph[v.] 22.] she asked her husband’s consent about it. #footnote[4\. Clothing.]

4\. What is noted of Hannah, that she brought her son “a little robe” each year “when she went with her husband,”#footnote[1 Sam 2:19.] shows that she did not do it without her husband’s consent. Women are for the most part inclined to dress their children up above their husband’s position and calling, and therefore there is good reason that in this they should be governed by their husbands.

#emph[Objection.] What if husbands are more eager than wives to have their children dressed vainly and unbecomingly?

#emph[Answer.] A wife must do what she can to hinder it. If she can in no way prevail with him, she, because of her subjection, is much more excused than he could be if he allowed his wife to have her will in this.

5\. The law that lays the charge on the husband to give #footnote[5\. Portions. Deut 21:15, #emph[etc.]] such and such portions to his children, and the corresponding practice of husbands from time to time, show that the wife has no power of herself to order them.

=== §. 40. #emph[Of a wife’s subjection to her husband about ordering servants and animals.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 18.]

If wives must have their husbands’ consent in ordering and disposing of their children, who come out of their own womb, much more must they have it for their servants.

They must not take in or turn out servants against their husbands’ mind. In this point, as in many others, Sarah showed her wifely obedience, in that #footnote[Gen 16:5.] she would not deal harshly with her maid, though she was provoked, much less #footnote[Gen 21:10.] put her out of doors, until she had made the matter known to her husband. Though she failed in the manner, yet in the thing itself she is a good example. It is further noted and approved in the #footnote[2 Kgs 4:22.] Shunammite that she asked her husband’s consent about sending a servant with her.

My meaning is not that wives who have servants allowed them to attend on them should ask their husbands’ consent whenever they have occasion to use them. For their husbands, by allowing them men to attend them, show their will and consent that they may use them as they see occasion. My meaning is that they should not use and employ their servants in such things as they know their husbands would dislike, unless they can gain their husbands’ consent.

Against these particulars of children and servants it may be objected that wives are parents of their children as well as husbands, and mistresses of servants as well as husbands are masters, and therefore have altogether as great a power over them as their husbands.

#emph[Answer.] Indeed, if the husband’s authority does not come between, that may be granted in the relation between her and them. But since her power is subordinate to her husband’s, she does not have so great a power in relation to him; and the power of a wife that we are now speaking of is directly in relation to her husband.

The same may be said of their animals and livestock, a particular point also noted in the example of the Shunammite. Having occasion to use an animal, she went to her husband and said, “Please send me … one of the donkeys.” #footnote[2 Kgs 4:22.]

=== §. 41. #emph[Of a wife’s subjection in receiving strangers, traveling from home, and making vows.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 18.]

If wives must not use the things belonging to the house at their pleasure, much less may they bring strangers into the house and receive them without or against their husbands’ consent. The good Shunammite,#footnote[2 Kgs 4:10.] so often named as a precedent for good wives, first asked her husband’s consent before she lodged a prophet of the Lord.

The same pattern is also commended to wives to move them not to travel from home without their husbands’ consent. For though that good wife had a very weighty and just occasion #footnote[2 Kgs 4:22.] to go to the prophet, yet she would not go before she knew her husband’s mind.

As for a wife’s power to make vows: since the law gives a husband power to annul her vow when he knows of it,#footnote[Num 30:9.] it implies that she ought to have his consent in making it, at least if she wants it to stand; and she ought to want that, or else she mocks God.

I have thought it good to mention these particular points to illustrate a wife’s subjection, because all of them are grounded on God’s word. Many others might be added to them, but these are sufficient.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of faults contrary to a wife’s subjection in doing things without or against their husbands’ consent.]

Now let us consider the usual vices and faults contrary to these duties. The general sum of them all is for a wife to take it on herself to do what she pleases, whether her husband wills it or not, either not wanting him to know what she does, or not caring though it is against his mind and will. Of this sort are

1\. Those who secretly take money out of their husbands’ cabinets, #footnote[1\. Secretly stealing their husbands’ goods.] tills or other such places where he keeps it, never telling him of it, nor wanting him to know it; likewise those who in the same way take wares out of the shop, grain out of the granary, sheep out of the flock, or any other goods, to sell and make money of, or to give away, or to use some other way, so that their husbands will never know, if they can help it. Such wives sin heinously in this, and that in many respects.

First, they disobey the ordinance of God in a main branch of their particular calling, which is #emph[subjection.]

2\. They repay badly the care and pains their husbands take for their good. Many such wives repay evil for good, which is a devilish quality.

3\. They are often a means of wasting and impoverishing their husbands’ estate.

4\. By this they show themselves no better than #footnote[#emph[Si vxor ex bonis communibus sine viri licentia saltem praesumpta accipiat, verè furtum committit. Greg. Sayr. clau. reg. l.] 9. #emph[c.] 16. \[If a wife takes from the common goods without her husband’s leave, at least presumed, she truly commits theft.\]] pilfering thieves. All that can be justly and truly said for their right in the common goods cannot defend them from the guilt of theft. They are the more dangerous the more they are trusted and the less they are suspected; and their deed is the more heinous the dearer their husbands ought to be to them.

5\. They are a very bad example to the other inferiors in the house. For a man seldom has a deceitful wife without some of the children or servants being made accessory to it, being made her instruments to take the goods and dispose of them as she orders; and so they are made unfaithful.

6\. They make themselves slaves to their own children and servants, whom they dare not displease for fear they should tell what was done.

7\. They teach their children and servants to be thieves. For besides that those whom their mistresses use to steal for them are by that made accessory to their sin, they will also steal for themselves when their mistresses will not know. So, what with the wife’s stealing one way and the children’s or servants’ another way, a man’s estate may be wasted like dew before the sun, and he not know which way it went.

2\. Those who will have whatever allowance they think best for #footnote[2\. Taking what allowance they please.] themselves and the family, and scornfully say, #emph[They will not live on what their husbands provide: they know best what allowance is fittest for the family, and that it will have.] Many will make their husbands’ ears ring with it, and indeed make the whole house (if not the street too) ring with it, if they think their allowance does not match the utmost extent of their husbands’ estate. This impatience and insolence, as it crosses God’s ordinance, so it makes life miserable for them both.

3\. Those who pamper, dress or in any way bring up their children #footnote[3\. Ordering children against their husbands’ mind.] otherwise than their husbands would, even to their husbands’ grief and dishonor: keeping them at home when their husbands, for their better education, would have them away from home. As these sin in hindering the good of their children, so they also sin in not yielding to their husbands. #footnote[4\. Ordering servants against their husbands’ mind.]

4\. Those who will have their own will about servants, taking in and putting out whom they please and when they please; using some servants whom they find suit their own purpose, to the harm of their husbands; and carrying themselves so sharply #footnote[5\. Lending out their husbands’ livestock.] and shrewishly toward others who serve their husbands’ purpose that a good, trusty, faithful servant cannot stay long in the house.

5\. Those who secretly lend out their husbands’ horses or other #footnote[6\. Feasting when their husbands will not know it. #emph[Absente marito compotationes domi ne excita. Greg. Naz. ad Olymp.] \[When your husband is away, do not stir up drinking parties at home.\]] such animals, caring more to please a vain friend than to please a good husband. This fault is all the greater when it is done to the husband’s loss and harm.

6\. Those who are most merry and festive when their husbands are farthest away and cannot know it. Solomon sets it down as a mark of a harlot, #footnote[Prov 7:19.] to deck out her house and to look for guests when her husband “has gone on a long journey.” Then she ought to be most solitary, and by abstaining from merry gatherings to show that nothing can dampen her mirth more than the absence of her husband.

7\. Those who think their houses a prison to them, who cannot #footnote[7\. Roaming about at their own pleasure. #emph[Noli pedem tuum limine crebro efferre. etc. Naz. loc. citat.] \[Do not often set your foot over the threshold, etc.\]] stay at home long. They think they have power to go when and where they will, and to stay out as long as they please, whatever their husbands think of it. #footnote[1 Tim 5:13.] The apostle lays this down as a mark of a wanton wife and an idle homemaker: “They … learn to be idle,” he says, “going from house to house.” Therefore in #footnote[Titus 2:5.] another place he exhorts them to be “keepers at home.” The wise man goes further, and makes this another mark of a harlot, that “her feet do not remain at home,”#footnote[Prov 7:11.] which we may see borne out in the Levite’s adulterous wife,#footnote[Judg 19:2.] whose fearful end was a stamp of God’s judgment on such loose wickedness.

8\. Those who do not care how or to what they bind themselves #footnote[8\. Binding themselves without their husbands’ consent.] without their husbands’ consent or knowledge. Those especially offend in this who, being seduced by Jesuits, priests or friars, take the sacrament, and on that bind themselves by solemn vow and oath never to read an English Bible, nor any Protestant books, nor even to go to any of their churches or to hear any of their sermons; and most of all those who enter some popish nunnery and vow never to return to their husbands again.

#emph[Objection.] Hannah#footnote[1 Sam 1:11.] vowed her child to God without her husband’s consent; why may they not much more vow themselves to God?

#emph[Answer.] She was certainly persuaded that her husband would not be against it, and so she had an implicit consent. This may well be gathered, because she afterward made it known to him, as the #footnote[שמואל #emph[componitur ex diuersis vocibus] \[is made up of separate words\] שאלתי אתו מאל #emph[Petiui eum a Deo,] I have asked for him from God.] name given to the child, and #footnote[1 Sam 1:22.] Hannah’s words, “I will take him to appear before the #smallcaps[Lord] and to stay there permanently,” and her husband’s answer, “May the #smallcaps[Lord] confirm His word,”#footnote[#emph[v.] 23.] and #footnote[1 Sam 2:19.] his going up with her when the child was dedicated to the Lord, all show.

So much for the first branch of a wife’s submission, in abstaining from doing things without her husband’s consent. The second follows: in doing the things he requires.
